@startuml 01_data_structures

title Core Data Structures - the sol_data dict pattern, OptimizationVariable

note as N0
  UPDATED: the typed TrajectoryState/ErrorDynamics/ErrorState
  NamedTuple pytrees this diagram used to describe were never
  built this way. What actually shipped: a single flat, mutable
  dict called sol_data, threaded through every pipeline stage.

  Every stage method has the same shape:
    (problem_def, sol_data) -> dict
  It reads whatever keys it needs, adds/overwrites keys for
  whatever it computed, and returns the same dict (mutated in
  place - JAX is fine with dict mutation under jit/vmap/jacfwd,
  same as it was fine with NamedTuple._replace()).

  Why: the math is still evolving (e.g. higher-order state
  transition tensors were being discussed as a future addition
  at time of writing) and the set of fields a given pipeline
  needs is not yet stable. Pre-declaring a typed schema forces
  guessing field names/defaults ahead of time; a flat dict lets
  a new field show up with a one-line sol_data['new_key'] = ...
  wherever it's actually computed, with no upstream type to
  touch. No docstring enumerates "which keys each stage
  populates" by design - read the stage's own body.
end note

class OptimizationVariable {
  +name : str
  +size : int
  +lower : array
  +upper : array
  +value : array      ' default value
  +guess_fn : callable ' (key) -> array, random init guess
}

note right of OptimizationVariable
  Still a genuine typed NamedTuple (src/problem/data_structures.py) -
  this is the one exception to the flat-dict pattern, and
  deliberately so: it's build-time-only (never enters traced
  code, never flows through evaluate()), so the "don't know the
  future schema" problem doesn't apply to it.

  One instance per addVarGroup() call. TOP.variables() returns
  list[OptimizationVariable]; TOP.to_pyoptsparse() turns the
  list into the actual optprob.addVarGroup(...) calls.
end note

note as N1
  Typical sol_data key growth through StochasticTOP.evaluate()
  (src/optimization/trajectory_optimization_problem.py):

    sol_data = dict(inputs)   ' U_arc_hst, X0, Xf, alpha, beta, gain_weights, ...

    sol_data = propagator.propagate(problem_def, sol_data)
      + X_hst, t_hst, t_node_bound
      + (stochastic only) A_hst, B_hst, A_arc_hst, B_arc_hst, A_postinsert

    sol_data = gain_param.compute_gains(problem_def, sol_data)
      + K_arc_hst

    sol_data = error_propagator.propagate_cov(problem_def, sol_data)
      + true_state:      P_hst, P_U_arc_hst
      + true_state_sqrt:  Saug_hst, KS_arc_hst
      + estimated_state:  Paug_hst, P_U_arc_hst, L_arc_hst, H_hst, P_v_hst

    J = objective(sol_data)                       ' reads whatever keys it needs
    for c in constraints(): c.evaluate(problem_def, sol_data)  ' same
end note

@enduml
